Hence, we have that
\begin{align}
\norm{ \UpD}{2}=\sup\nolimits_{{\bf z}\in \mathbb{R}^N: \norm{ {\bf z}}{2}=1}\sqrt{\sum\nolimits_{i=1,\ldots,N} 
\sum\nolimits_{j=1,\ldots, N} \UpD(i,j)\cdot{\bf z}(j)} \enspace.
\end{align}
Since the entries of $\UpD$ are assumed to be non-negative, it is elementary to verify that supremum, above, is achieved for 
 ${\bf z}$ whose entries are non-negative. 

Let ${\bf x}\in \mathbb{R}^{N}$ be such that $\norm{ {\bf x}}{2}=1$ and $\norm{ \UpD \cdot {\bf x}}{2}=\norm{ \UpD}{2} $, while
